---
import SeoPage from '../../layouts/SeoPage.astro';
import { omLimits } from '../../facts/seo-facts.mjs';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Can I run an AI meeting notetaker fully on my own hardware?',
    a: 'Mostly. Local faster-whisper transcribes on your CPU with no key, and summaries can go to a model you host yourself through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint such as Ollama, or be switched off. The call itself still runs on Google Meet or Microsoft Teams, and the bot joins it from your server.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does it need to run?',
    a: 'Docker with the Compose plugin. Plan for about 1 vCPU and 1.5 GB of RAM per meeting recorded at the same time, plus about 1 GB for the web, worker and database containers. These are planning figures from the docs, not measurements.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it ready for production?',
    a: 'It is pre-release. There is no tagged version or published image yet, so you build from source for now, and breaking changes are expected until v0.1.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I do not want to run a server. What then?',
    a: 'We can run the same software for you from EUR 9 a month, priced by concurrent meetings. You still bring your own AI keys and your own Google and Microsoft apps.',
  },
];
---

<SeoPage
  title="Self-hosted AI meeting notetaker for Meet and Teams | OpenMinutes"
  description="A self-hosted AI meeting notetaker: a bot joins Google Meet and Microsoft Teams, and you keep the audio and transcripts. Open source, Docker, bring your own AI."
  h1="A self-hosted AI meeting notetaker"
  lede="OpenMinutes is a meeting notetaker you run on your own machine. A bot joins your Google Meet and Microsoft Teams meetings, and the audio, transcripts and summaries end up in your database."
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Use cases', path: '/compare' }, { name: 'Self-hosted notetaker', path: '/use/self-hosted-ai-meeting-notetaker' }]}
  {faq}
  related={[
    { href: '/compare', label: 'Compare with hosted notetakers' },
    { href: '/use/gdpr-meeting-notes', label: 'Meeting notes and GDPR' },
    { href: '/use/open-source-meeting-recorder', label: 'Open source meeting recorder' },
    { href: '/docs/getting-started', label: 'Getting started' },
    { href: '/hosting', label: 'Hosted OpenMinutes' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="callout callout-warning">
    <p>
      <strong>Status</strong>
      OpenMinutes is pre-release. There is no tagged version or published image yet, so for now you build from source with
      <code>docker compose up -d --build</code>. Read <a href="/docs/roadmap">the roadmap</a> for what works today.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What runs where</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col">Where it runs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Web app, worker, bot and database</th><td>Containers on your machine, one Postgres and one volume. <a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting started</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Audio and transcripts</th><td>Your volume, or your own S3-compatible bucket. You set how long audio is kept. <a href="/docs/storage">Storage</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Transcription</th><td>Local faster-whisper on your CPU with no key, or OpenAI, Groq, Deepgram or AssemblyAI with your own key. <a href="/docs/providers">Providers</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Summaries</th><td>Off by default. Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (including Ollama) or Anthropic, with your key.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Calendar</th><td>Google Calendar and Outlook through your own OAuth client and Azure app, or just paste a link.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>A fully local setup</h2>
  <p>
    Use the bundled <code>local</code> transcriber and leave summaries off (or use Ollama on the same host). Then the only outbound
    traffic is the bot joining the meeting and any calendar or mail provider you connect. No telemetry, no account.
    Google or Microsoft still host the call, and participants still see the bot. See <a href="/docs/faq">the FAQ</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What it costs</h2>
  <p>
    The software is free. You pay for the machine and your AI provider: hosted transcription is $0.04 to $0.36 per hour of audio
    (October 2026, <a href="/docs/providers">table</a>), local Whisper is free. Or <a href="/hosting">we host it from EUR 9 a month</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to choose it</h2>
  <ul>
    {omLimits.map((l: { text: string; href: string; label: string }) => <li>{l.text} <a href={l.href}>{l.label}</a></li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>
    If those are dealbreakers, a hosted notetaker may fit better. See the <a href="/compare">comparisons</a>. Among the hosted
    products we checked, we found no self-hosted edition on their public pages.
  </p>
</SeoPage>
